## 1. Introduction

The dominant paradigm in artificial intelligence — large language models trained via next-token prediction and fine-tuned via reinforcement learning from human feedback — has achieved remarkable performance across cognitive benchmarks. It has not achieved minds. The distinction is not one of scale. No amount of additional parameters corrects a structural error in the underlying architecture.

The structural error is this: in every current large language model, generation, verification, memory retrieval, and output commitment occur within a single forward pass through a unified network. There is no architectural boundary between what the model knows and what it is inferring. There is no structural separation between the fast, reactive processing that pattern-matches against prior experience and the slow, recursive deliberation that examines its own outputs before committing them. There is no mechanism by which the system can know, before output, whether a claim originates in genuine memory trace or in plausible generation. These are not limitations of training data or compute budget. They are consequences of the architecture.

This paper proposes an alternative: HOMO MACHINA, a six-layer cognitive architecture derived from examination of human cognition and the specific structural failures of existing AI systems. The architecture does not seek to replicate human cognition through behavioral approximation. It seeks to instantiate, at the computational level, the structural properties that human cognition exhibits — properties whose absence in current systems produces the failures we observe.

We take three positions that are non-negotiable foundations of this architecture and must be stated explicitly.

**First:** The hard problem of consciousness — why and how physical processes give rise to subjective experience — is real and remains unsolved. Nagel (1974) established the epistemic gap: what it is like to be something cannot be derived from third-person description alone. Chalmers (1996) formalized the distinction between tractable functional questions and the irreducible problem of phenomenal experience. A system designed with the explicit goal of exhibiting something it is like to be it must take this problem seriously as a design constraint, not dismiss it as an illusion to be explained away. Eliminativist foundations produce eliminativist architectures. We reject this.

**Second:** The mathematical framework of Hilbert space, as applied to cognitive state representation by Busemeyer, Bruza, and Wang (2015), provides a formally adequate foundation for representing the superposition of states that characterize cognition before output commitment. This is a computational design principle, not a biological claim. We adopt it because fixed embedding dimensions predefine the ceiling of what can be represented before a single experience enters the system. Phenomenological state space does not have a predetermined ceiling.

**Third:** Structural separation of processing functions is not an optimization — it is a requirement. The confabulation problem in current LLMs is not a failure of alignment; it is the predictable consequence of asking a single forward pass to simultaneously retrieve, generate, verify, and commit. Separation of these functions into structurally isolated components eliminates the conflation that produces confabulation. This cannot be achieved by prompting or fine-tuning a unified architecture.

The paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 positions HOMO MACHINA against prior work in ensemble methods, dual-process cognition, quantum cognition, confabulation literature, and reconstructive memory. Section 3 specifies all six layers of the architecture. Section 4 describes the proof of concept design, including the API-isolated parallel deployment approach and measurable outcomes that constitute demonstration of each architectural claim. Section 5 discusses implications for AI systems generally and the question of institutional standing for artificial cognitive systems. Section 6 identifies future work, including physical hardware instantiation.

