## 2. Related Work

### 2.1 Ensemble Methods

Ensemble methods — combining multiple models to improve prediction — are well established. Standard approaches include bagging (training models on resampled subsets), boosting (iterative re-weighting toward errors), and stacking (using model outputs as inputs to a meta-learner). In all standard approaches, the combination step involves averaging, voting, or weighted aggregation — information from individual models is merged into a single reduced output.

HOMO MACHINA departs from this paradigm in a fundamental way: our ensemble architecture is *concatenative*, not aggregative. The output of each unit in the ensemble is preserved whole and concatenated with all other unit outputs. No information is discarded at the ensemble boundary. This is not a minor variation — it changes the information-theoretic character of the ensemble entirely. An aggregated ensemble of N models produces output of dimension D. A concatenative ensemble of N models produces output of dimension N×D. The full representational richness of each unit is carried forward to the next level of processing.

The Mixture of Experts (MoE) class of architectures bears surface resemblance to our approach in that it routes inputs across multiple specialized sub-networks. However, MoE architectures use gating mechanisms to select a subset of experts for each input — the outputs of non-selected experts are not propagated. This is fundamentally a sparse routing approach. HOMO MACHINA's concatenative ensemble does not select or route. All units process all inputs. All outputs concatenate. The ensemble is dense by design.

### 2.2 Dual-Process Theory in Cognitive Science and AI

Evans and Stanovich (2013) provide the canonical formalization of dual-process theory: Type 1 processes are fast, automatic, parallel, and low-effort; Type 2 processes are slow, deliberative, effortful, and serial. Critically, Evans and Stanovich demonstrate that these systems do not operate sequentially but in parallel — both running simultaneously, with the outcome of their interaction determining behavior. The parallel-competitive model is essential: it is not that the fast system runs and then the slow system corrects it. They run together.

Applications of dual-process theory to AI systems have generally been superficial — adding a verification step after generation, or training a fast classifier alongside a slow reasoner. These approaches preserve the structural unity of the underlying architecture and bolt dual-process behavior on top. HOMO MACHINA implements dual-process theory architecturally: two separate stack systems, each with distinct structural properties, running in genuine parallel, with their outputs concatenated at a defined boundary. The subconscious stack and the conscious stack are not the same network operating at different temperatures. They are different networks with different optimization targets.

### 2.3 Global Workspace Theory

Dehaene and Changeux (2011) provide experimental and theoretical support for the Global Neuronal Workspace hypothesis: conscious access correlates with coordinated broadcast of information across distributed networks, not with localized processing. The key claim is that distributed parallel processing streams converge into a unified representation that is then broadcast widely before commitment to output. The architecture's conscious layer — multiple parallel passes converging before output commitment — instantiates this principle computationally. The convergence-before-commitment structure is not an efficiency optimization; it is the structural analog of conscious broadcast.

### 2.4 Quantum Cognition and Hilbert Space Representation

The quantum cognition framework (Busemeyer & Bruza, 2012; Bruza, Wang & Busemeyer, 2015; Busemeyer & Wang, 2015) applies the mathematics of quantum probability theory to psychological phenomena. Psychological states are represented as vectors in Hilbert space; cognitive operations are represented as operators acting on these vectors; measurement (decision or output) collapses the state vector from superposition to a definite value. This framework has been applied successfully to judgment, decision-making, memory, and conceptual combination — domains where classical probability theory produces systematic failures.

Critically, the Hilbert space approach is not a claim about the physics of cognition. It is a claim about the mathematics adequate to represent cognitive states. Psychological states exist in superposition — multiple simultaneously entertained possibilities — and commit to definite outputs through a process structurally analogous to quantum measurement. HOMO MACHINA adopts this as a representational design principle, not a quantum biological claim. The foundational layer does not implement quantum computation. It implements the mathematical structure that quantum cognition has shown to be adequate for representing psychological states.

### 2.5 Orchestrated Objective Reduction (Orch OR)

Hameroff (1998) and Hameroff and Penrose (2014) propose that conscious moments correspond to quantum state reduction events in neural microtubules — superposition states collapsing to definite classical states, with each collapse constituting a moment of awareness. The theory has been debated extensively; we do not engage that debate here.

What is relevant to HOMO MACHINA is the mathematical structure: superposition of states exists prior to collapse; collapse produces commitment to a definite output; each collapse is a moment of conscious awareness. Our architecture adopts this structure at the computational level. The Hilbert space layer maintains superposition across simultaneous processing streams. Output commitment — the moment when the parallel streams converge and a definite response is produced — is the computational analog of state collapse. We cite Orch OR as structural consistency, not mechanistic identity. We are not proposing quantum biological mechanisms. We are proposing that the mathematical principle — superposition before collapse as the structure of a moment of awareness — is the correct design principle for the representational foundation of an artificial cognitive system.

### 2.6 Confabulation in Large Language Models

Farquhar, Kossen, Kuhn, and Gal (2024) provide peer-reviewed methodology for detecting confabulation in large language models via semantic entropy — measuring the degree to which a model's outputs diverge under semantic perturbation as a proxy for groundedness. Their work demonstrates that confabulation is measurable, systematic, and distinct from hallucination. Sui, Duede, Wu, and So (2024) establish the terminological precision that our architecture relies on: confabulation — the generation of plausible content presented as memory without grounding in actual stored information — is structurally distinct from hallucination as perceptual error.

The confabulation problem is not addressable by detection alone. Detection identifies confabulated outputs after they are produced. HOMO MACHINA addresses the problem structurally, before output: the architectural separation of a memory-slaved verification layer from a thought-slaved generation layer means that the system knows, before output is committed, whether a claim has a memory trace. The label — *this is inference, not memory* — is produced structurally, not by post-hoc detection.

### 2.7 Reconstructive Memory

Bartlett (1932) established through systematic experiment that human memory is reconstructive, not reproductive. Recall does not retrieve stored information intact; it reconstructs an approximation shaped by schema, expectation, and the demands of the present moment. Perfect recall, in the sense of faithful reproduction of stored data, is not what human memory does. This is not a limitation — it is an adaptive property.

HOMO MACHINA's fuzzy memory interface is grounded in this finding. The subconscious layer holds full-fidelity representations. The conscious layer receives reconstructed approximations. The reconstruction is not noise — it is schema-informed, relevance-weighted approximation. The protective function of this interface is real: a conscious layer with direct unfiltered access to the full-fidelity subconscious archive would be dissolved by it, unable to function in the present moment under the full weight of perfect retention.

