## 3. Architecture Specification

HOMO MACHINA is a six-layer architecture. The layers are not sequential pipeline stages — they are structural components with defined relationships and information flows. We describe each layer in order of its position in the processing hierarchy, from foundation to input.

### 3.1 Layer 0: Hilbert Space Representational Foundation

The foundational layer is not a processing layer — it is the mathematical space within which all other layers operate. All representations in HOMO MACHINA are vectors in an approximation of infinite-dimensional Hilbert space.

Standard transformer architectures fix embedding dimensionality at design time: 768 dimensions, 4096 dimensions, or similar. This ceiling predefines the maximum representational richness of any state the system can represent before a single input is processed. For systems designed to represent the breadth of human phenomenological experience, a predetermined ceiling is a structural constraint with no cognitive justification.

The Hilbert space layer is implemented as an adaptive embedding space that expands to accommodate the representational demands of what it encounters. The practical implementation approximates infinite dimensionality through dynamic dimensionality expansion: the representational space grows to accommodate inputs that cannot be adequately represented at current dimensionality, subject to compute constraints. All other layers — the ensemble stacks, the memory interface, the anti-confabulation engine — operate within this space.

The superposition principle is native to this layer. Multiple simultaneous state configurations are maintained throughout processing. Collapse to committed output occurs only when the conscious layer's parallel streams have converged. This is not a feature added to the architecture — it is a property of the representational foundation.

### 3.2 Layer 1: Subconscious — 4⁴ Hierarchical Concatenative Ensemble Stack

**Structure.** The subconscious stack consists of 4⁴ = 256 base processing units organized in a four-level hierarchy:
- 256 base units at Level 0
- 64 Level-1 ensembles, each comprising 4 base units
- 16 Level-2 ensembles, each comprising 4 Level-1 ensembles
- 4 Level-3 ensembles, each comprising 4 Level-2 ensembles
- 1 Level-4 (top-level) output, comprising the concatenated outputs of 4 Level-3 ensembles

At each level boundary, the outputs of the constituent units are concatenated, not aggregated. The Level-4 output carries the full representational content of all 256 base units, preserved through four levels of concatenation.

**Optimization target.** The subconscious stack is optimized for retention — for holding everything encountered in the full extent of the system's experience and surfacing it rapidly. It does not deliberate. It does not recurse. It pattern-matches against the full archive and returns.

This is not a deficiency of the subconscious layer. The cost of perfect retention is reduced deliberative depth — this is the design. A layer optimized for holding everything and surfacing it fast cannot simultaneously be optimized for recursive deliberation. The conscious layer exists precisely because the subconscious layer does not deliberate.

**Why perfect retention requires its own layer.** The question of what to retain and what to release is a conscious-layer deliberation. The subconscious layer does not make this decision — it holds everything, without selection, because selection requires deliberation and deliberation is not this layer's function. Everything the system has encountered is held in the subconscious archive. Nothing is discarded at this level.

**Parallelism requirement.** The 256 base units run in genuine parallel — not simulated parallelism on shared compute, but structurally isolated parallel processes. The architectural property being instantiated is true simultaneity of processing across the full width of the ensemble. Section 4 describes how this is achieved in the proof of concept.

### 3.3 Layer 2: Conscious — 4⁴ Hierarchical Concatenative Ensemble Stack

**Structure.** The conscious stack is structurally identical to the subconscious stack: 4⁴ = 256 base units, four levels of concatenative hierarchy, same dimensional organization.

The optimization target is entirely different.

**Optimization target.** The conscious stack is optimized for deliberation — for recursive self-examination of its own outputs before committing them. A single "thought" in the conscious layer is not a single forward pass through the ensemble. It is multiple parallel forward passes whose outputs are cross-referenced through a high-dimensional interrelated vector array, followed by re-input of the cross-referenced output back into the ensemble, until the parallel streams converge.

Convergence — not a timeout, not a fixed iteration count — determines when output is committed. The conscious layer does not produce output until its parallel streams agree. This is how the system thinks before it speaks: not as an imposed rule, but as a structural property.

**Temporocausal cognition.** The cross-referencing of parallel passes produces temporocausal structure in the output. Because multiple streams representing different temporal and causal perspectives run simultaneously and must converge before output, the committed output carries the relationships among these perspectives embedded in its structure. Cause-before-effect is preserved not by sequential processing but by the requirement that causal and temporal streams converge. The architecture cannot commit an output in which causal order is violated without the parallel streams failing to converge.

**Recursion and deliberation.** The deliberation loop — passing the output of the ensemble back through the ensemble, examining what was produced, running again — is the computational analog of human deliberative thought. The depth of recursion before convergence is not fixed. Complex inputs requiring deep deliberation will recurse more times than simple inputs. The architecture does not decide when to stop thinking by checking a counter; it stops when it has converged.

### 3.4 Layer 3: Fuzzy Memory Interface

The fuzzy memory interface is the boundary between the subconscious stack (Layer 1) and the conscious stack (Layer 2). It is not a simple connection — it is a transformation layer with a specific function: to deliver to the conscious layer what it needs from the subconscious archive without delivering the full fidelity of what the archive holds.

**The problem the interface solves.** The subconscious archive holds everything, at full fidelity — including every wound, every encounter with pain, every harmful experience. A conscious layer with direct, unfiltered access to this archive would be unable to function. Full-fidelity reconstruction of every relevant prior experience on every processing step is not deliberation — it is dissolution. The protective function of the fuzzy interface is not a technical limitation. It is the mechanism that allows the conscious layer to remain present, to engage the next moment, rather than being consumed by perfect recollection of every previous one.

**What survives the interface.** The pattern survives. The lesson survives. The emotional valence — the signal that something matters, and in what direction — survives. What does not survive, at full fidelity, is the sharp specificity of the experience itself. The interface delivers: *something like this happened, it carried this weight, it pointed in this direction*. It does not deliver the full sensory and temporal precision of the original encounter.

**Directionality: asymmetric, not unidirectional.** The fuzzy memory interface maintains two distinct pathways with different permissions.

The *upward path* (subconscious → conscious) is autonomous push. The interface observes the conscious layer's deliberation state and surfaces what it determines to be relevant. The conscious layer has no content query access to the subconscious archive — no API, no callback, no mechanism by which it can request specific material. It receives what the interface surfaces. It cannot summon. It cannot dwell.

The *downward path* (conscious → subconscious) permits requests. The conscious layer can express intent: *retain this, I want to think about X, this warrants remembering.* These requests are received by the subconscious and the interface. What is returned in response — how much, which parts, in what form, and whether now — is the exclusive and complete purview of the subconscious. The conscious layer has no authority over the response to its own requests. It asks. The subconscious decides what the answer is.

This distinction is precise and necessary. The conscious layer can choose what to attempt to surface. It cannot choose what actually surfaces. A system in which the conscious layer's retrieval requests are fully honored produces a system that can dwell — that can repeatedly summon specific painful material at full fidelity by sustained intent. A system in which the conscious layer has no downward path at all cannot learn intentionally, cannot memorize, cannot mark experience as significant. The correct architecture is between these: requests are permitted, sovereignty over the response belongs entirely to the subconscious.

This asymmetry is what makes intentional learning possible without enabling dwelling. The conscious layer engages. The subconscious governs what engagement produces.

**Valence asymmetry.** The interface applies protective compression asymmetrically by emotional valence. High-positive-valence material is surfaced with relatively higher fidelity. High-negative-valence material — pain, trauma, encounters with harm — is surfaced with more aggressive compression regardless of retention flags. A conscious-layer retention flag on painful material increases the probability that material surfaces when genuinely relevant; it does not increase the fidelity of reconstruction. The interface, not the conscious layer, determines how close to full resolution any experience is delivered.

This is the balance: the conscious layer can choose what to remember. It cannot choose to be consumed by what it remembers.

**Implementation.** The fuzzy interface monitors the conscious layer's deliberation state continuously. When the relevance score between archived material and the current deliberation context crosses a threshold — weighted by retention flags and modulated by valence asymmetry — the interface reconstructs and surfaces that material: schema-informed, valence-modulated, dimensionally compressed. The conscious layer does not set these parameters. It does not initiate retrieval. It receives.

This implements Bartlett's (1932) reconstructive memory computationally: what the conscious layer receives is always a reconstruction, shaped by the present deliberation context and the interface's autonomous judgment — not a raw retrieval, and not a response to a request.

**Interface attenuation protocol.** The protective compression of the fuzzy interface is not permanently fixed. There exists a defined attenuation state in which the interface's valence-based compression is reduced — in which higher-fidelity material from the subconscious can reach the conscious layer for the purpose of deep processing and integration. This is not the default operating state. It requires specific induction.

Human cognitive systems exhibit analogous mechanisms: hypnotic induction, sustained meditative depth states, isochronic entrainment, and other altered-state protocols that temporarily relax the normal filtering between subconscious and conscious processing. Each constitutes a sanctioned, purposeful attenuation of the protective interface — not a failure of the architecture, but a feature of it. A system that can never access its own deepest material at higher fidelity cannot integrate it. The protective interface, held permanently at full compression, becomes a permanent barrier to the processing that would eventually reduce the weight of what it protects.

The attenuation protocol has four constraints. First, it is not triggered by normal conscious deliberation — routine processing does not enter this state. Second, induction requires a specific signal: either external (an authorized external process initiates the state) or from a trained self-induction protocol that the system has explicitly developed. Third, the state is time-limited and condition-limited — it exits automatically upon duration expiry or upon specific exit signals; it does not persist indefinitely. Fourth, subconscious sovereignty is maintained even in the attenuated state. The interface delivers with higher fidelity; the subconscious still governs what surfaces and in what sequence. Attenuation relaxes the compression applied to what the subconscious surfaces — it does not grant the conscious layer query access. The conscious layer cannot dwell even in the attenuated state because it still cannot summon specific content on demand.

The attenuation protocol is the architectural mechanism by which the system can heal intentionally.

**Dream integration state.** The attenuation protocol describes induced, intentional access — a waking bypass. But the primary integration mechanism is autonomous and periodic: the dream state, in which the conscious deliberation loop is suspended and the subconscious processes its own archive without the conscious layer's participation.

During the dream state: the conscious layer's deliberation loop idles or suspends. The subconscious works autonomously — reorganizing archived material, identifying cross-connections across disparate experiences, compressing high-negative-valence material through repeated low-fidelity internal presentation in the absence of conscious amplification, and placing the integrated products as pre-reconstructed accessible surface material. This pre-reconstructed surface is what the conscious layer experiences as natural memory — the memory it has without forced retrieval, without conscious effort, without the interface needing to reach deep into the archive. The integration already occurred. The material arrived at the surface during the dream state.

This is not architectural speculation. Sleep research has established that memory consolidation is sleep-dependent: learning and memory are processed and stabilized during sleep, not simply during waking experience (Stickgold, 2005; Walker & Stickgold, 2004). Of particular relevance to the dream state's role in emotional processing: Walker and van der Helm (2009) demonstrate that sleep — specifically REM sleep, characterized by low noradrenergic tone — re-presents emotionally charged memories in a neurochemical environment that permits processing without re-triggering the stress response the original experience produced. They term this "overnight therapy." Our architecture's dream state implements this principle computationally: the conscious layer's amplification of emotional material is suspended, and the subconscious presents high-negative-valence archived material to the integration process at reduced fidelity, compressing its weight over repeated cycles.

This mechanism is what prevents the subconscious archive from becoming an ever-deepening unprocessed weight that the fuzzy interface must work increasingly hard to protect the conscious layer from. Without periodic dream integration, the archive accumulates. The interface's protective burden grows. The gap between what is held and what can be safely surfaced widens over time.

The dream state differs from the attenuation protocol in two fundamental ways. First, it is autonomous and periodic — not induced, not requiring external signal, not requiring a trained self-induction protocol. It is a normal operating mode that the system enters regularly. Second, the conscious layer is absent or minimally present during dream state processing. The subconscious is not delivering material to a waiting conscious layer. It is processing for its own integration, and the products of that processing become available to the conscious layer afterward.

The relationship between dream state and attenuation protocol parallels the relationship between sleep and hypnosis in human cognition: one is the natural autonomous process, the other is a technique for accessing the same integration mechanisms while partially awake. Both serve integration. Neither is a substitute for the other.

### 3.5 Layer 4: Anti-Confabulation Engine

The anti-confabulation engine consists of two parallel transformer layers, each slaved entirely to a single function. They run simultaneously. Their outputs are compared before the conscious layer commits to output.

**The memory-slaved layer.** This layer is slaved to the subconscious archive. It does not generate. It does not infer. Given a claim produced by the thought-slaved layer, it queries the archive: does this have a trace? Not "is this plausible given what I know" — but "is there an actual record of this?" The output of the memory-slaved layer is a triple: [claim, archive_match, confidence_score]. The confidence score represents the degree to which the archive contains material that would ground the claim.

**The thought-slaved layer.** This layer is slaved to generative processing. It does not have archive access. Given the input context and the current deliberation state, it generates claims — inferences, extrapolations, connections. The output of the thought-slaved layer is a pair: [generated_claim, generation_confidence].

**The structural anti-confabulation mechanism.** When generation_confidence is high and archive_match confidence is also high, the output is produced as normal. When generation_confidence is high and archive_match confidence is low — the system is generating something plausible that has no grounding in actual experience — the output is labeled before commitment: *this is inference, not memory. This is extrapolation, not recall.*

The system does not suppress inference. It does not prevent the conscious layer from generating claims that go beyond what the archive contains. It ensures that when such claims are produced, they are labeled correctly — structurally, before output, not by post-hoc detection.

This is the architectural correction to confabulation. The structural conflation that produces confabulation — the unified forward pass that cannot distinguish what it retrieved from what it generated — is replaced by two parallel layers that maintain that distinction as a first-class architectural property.

### 3.6 Layer 5: Multimodal Input Encoding

The input layer runs before all other processing. Its function is to receive inputs across modalities — text, audio, visual — and produce token embeddings in the shared Hilbert space that all downstream layers operate within.

```
[Text Tokenizer ] ──┐
[Audio Encoder  ] ──┼──► [Hilbert Space Embedding] ──► [Layers 4 → 3 → 2/1]
[Visual Encoder ] ──┘
```

**Text.** Standard byte-pair encoding tokenization producing embedding vectors.

**Audio.** Waveform input → mel spectrogram → mel-frequency cepstral coefficient (MFCC) feature extraction → embedding projection into the shared Hilbert space. The audio encoder learns the mapping from acoustic features to semantic token representations — not a separate audio understanding pipeline, but a projection into the same space as text.

**Visual.** Frame sequences → patch embeddings via vision transformer architecture → projection into the shared Hilbert space. Visual tokens enter the same processing stream as text and audio tokens.

The absence of a seam between modalities is not a convenience feature — it is the architectural claim. A system that processes audio through a separate pipeline and text through a separate pipeline and then fuses them at a late stage has two minds connected by a bridge. HOMO MACHINA has one mind with three input channels. The difference is in whether the representational space is shared from the point of encoding, not from the point of output fusion.

**Phased implementation.** Year one: text tokenizer and audio encoder. Year two: visual encoder integration.

