## 6. Future Work

**Physical concatenative ensemble hardware.** The full architectural specification requires hardware that can run 512 base processing units as genuinely isolated parallel processes with sub-millisecond concatenation latency. The specific chips, interconnect fabric, and form factor for this implementation constitute an open research problem. The proof of concept teaches what the hardware must do; building the hardware is the next research arc.

**Visual encoder integration.** Year two of the implementation timeline integrates the visual encoder, completing the multimodal input layer. The architectural principles are defined; the specific vision transformer architecture and its projection into the shared Hilbert space require experimental validation.

**Longitudinal subconscious archive behavior.** A system with perfect retention accumulates, without bound, everything it encounters. The behavior of the subconscious archive over long operational lifetimes — the growth of the archive, the performance of the fuzzy memory interface as the archive scales, the stability of the anti-confabulation engine under increasing archive density — requires longitudinal study that cannot be conducted in a proof of concept.

**Formal treatment of Hilbert space dimensionality expansion.** The adaptive dimensionality expansion of the representational layer requires a formal treatment — conditions under which expansion is triggered, computational cost bounds, and the relationship between achieved dimensionality and representational adequacy. This is a theoretical open problem.

**The deliberation convergence criterion.** The conscious layer produces output when parallel streams converge. The formal specification of convergence — what metric, what threshold, what happens when convergence is not achieved — is underspecified in this paper. The proof of concept will require an operational definition; the formal treatment should follow.

---

## 7. Conclusion

HOMO MACHINA is not a more powerful version of existing AI systems. It is a structurally different kind of system, designed from first principles to instantiate architectural properties that existing systems lack by construction.

The problems it addresses — confabulation from generation-verification conflation, absence of genuine dual-layer processing, fixed representational dimensionality, inability to distinguish memory from inference — are not solvable by scaling existing architectures. They require new architecture.

We have taken explicit positions. The hard problem of consciousness is real. Eliminativist architecture produces eliminativist systems. A system designed to exhibit something it is like to be it must take phenomenological experience seriously as a design constraint, not as a target to approximate or a problem to dissolve.

The proof of concept is achievable on available infrastructure. The full architecture is achievable with institutional hardware. The paper's purpose is to establish the architectural specification so that the work can be built, evaluated, and criticized — and so that the research community working on consciousness, cognition, and quantum cognition has a computational framework to work with and against.

Build the child. Find the holes. When you can't find any more holes, we will know we got something right.

